/// Root of the SDK exception hierarchy.
///
/// Every error the SDK raises ultimately extends this single branded base, so
/// a caller can write one `on {{errorPrefix}}Exception catch (e)` and be
/// certain it covers all SDK-originated failures — API/HTTP errors
/// ([ApiException] and its typed subclasses), serialization failures
/// ([SerializationException]), and OAuth2 token/server errors raised during
/// authentication. The chain for a typed HTTP error is, for example:
/// `UnauthorizedException → ClientException → ApiException →
/// {{errorPrefix}}Exception`.
///
/// Dart has no checked exceptions and `Exception` is merely a marker
/// interface, so this is an abstract base class that `implements Exception`.
/// Concrete leaves extend it (directly or transitively) so that
/// `is {{errorPrefix}}Exception` holds up the whole chain.
abstract class {{errorPrefix}}Exception implements Exception {
  /// Const constructor so `const` subclass constructors (e.g. the typed
  /// [ApiException] leaves) remain valid.
  const {{errorPrefix}}Exception();

  /// The detail message describing what went wrong.
  String get message;

  @override
  String toString() => message;
}
